# Zustand

- Each store lives in its own file: global stores under `src/stores/`, feature-scoped stores in `src/features/<feature>/store.ts`.
- Don't merge unrelated domains into one large store (e.g. don't let `authStore` also hold `cartStore`'s state).
- Store shape: separate `state` and `actions` clearly within the same interface; name actions as verbs (`setUser`, `resetFilters`, `toggleSidebar`...).
- Use the `immer` middleware when state has nested/object structure, to avoid manual, error-prone mutation.
- Use the `persist` middleware only for state that must survive a reload (e.g. auth token, theme) — don't persist transient UI state (modal open, loading flags...).
- Selectors: when a component only needs part of the state, always use a selector (`useStore((s) => s.user)`) instead of pulling the whole store object, to avoid unnecessary re-renders.
- Don't trigger side effects (API calls) directly inside a store action if the project already uses React Query/SWR for server state — Zustand should only hold client/UI state; let the fetching library own server state.

# Reference pattern

```ts
interface CartState {
  items: CartItem[];
  addItem: (item: CartItem) => void;
  removeItem: (id: string) => void;
  clear: () => void;
}

export const useCartStore = create<CartState>()((set) => ({
  items: [],
  addItem: (item) => set((state) => ({ items: [...state.items, item] })),
  removeItem: (id) => set((state) => ({ items: state.items.filter((i) => i.id !== id) })),
  clear: () => set({ items: [] }),
}));
```
